<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Applies to every `dotnet test` in this repository, wired in by Directory.Build.props.

  TestSessionTimeout is here because a deadlocked test used to hang the suite
  indefinitely rather than fail it: nothing in dotnet test, the CI job or the local
  gate bounds a test run, so a stuck reader waits for as long as anybody lets it.

  Forty-five minutes, because the bound has to clear a loaded machine and not just an
  idle one. The Arbiter session took 14m06s idle and 17m05s with other work running
  beside it, which aborted the fifteen-minute bound this used to carry - and an
  aborted run is a failure the whole suite pays for, not a slow one. Headroom is
  cheap here and a false abort is not.

  A run past this bound is not a slow machine, it is a hang, and a hang that fails is
  worth more than one nobody can interrupt. Raise the bound rather than removing it.

  What an abort is worth is a separate question, and ./scripts/test-session.sh owns
  it: `dotnet test` reports an aborted run as "Passed!" with a partial total, so the
  verdict for a session comes from that script and never from the printed count.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestSessionTimeout>2700000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
